# The standard library

The library is a set of formula sheets written in Math++ itself and built into the program. Bring in a whole sheet with `![[…]]`, or just the names you need with a `Using` sentence.

## Constants

Using $\varphi, \gamma$ from [[std/constants.mpp]].

The golden ratio is exact, and Euler's constant is stored to 60 digits: $\varphi = ?$, $\gamma \approx ?$.

$$ \varphi^2 \overset{?}{=} \varphi + 1 $$

## Physics with units

Using $c, g_0, G$ from [[std/physics.mpp]].

$c = ?$, and the weight of a 70 kg person is $70\,\mathrm{kg} \cdot g_0 \approx ?$.

## Geometry and trigonometry

![[std/geometry.mpp]]

$A_{\text{circle}}(2) = ?$, $V_{\text{ball}}(3) = ?$, $V_{\text{cone}}(3, 4) = ?$.

![[std/trig.mpp]]

A triangle with sides 13, 14, 15 has area $A_{\text{Heron}}(13, 14, 15) = ?$, and the third side of a triangle with sides 3 and 4 at a right angle is $c_{\text{cos}}(3, 4, \tfrac{\pi}{2}) = ?$.

$$ \sin^2 x + \cos^2 x \overset{?}{=} 1 $$

## Finance

Using $A_{\text{compound}}, M_{\text{loan}}$ from [[std/finance.mpp]].

£1000 at 5% for two years: $A_{\text{compound}}(1000, 0.05, 1, 2) = ?$. The yearly payment on a 1000 loan over one year at 10%: $M_{\text{loan}}(1000, \tfrac{1}{10}, 1) = ?$.

## Functions across the fields

Textbook names work as soon as they are applied, with no import: $\tau(360) = ?$, $\sigma(28) = ?$, $\mu(30) = ?$, $\varphi(36) = ?$, $\zeta(2) = ?$, $J_0(1) \approx ?$, $B(\tfrac{1}{2}, \tfrac{1}{2}) = ?$.

## Reproducible randomness

%seed 42

With a seed, random samples come out the same every time: $\operatorname{runif}(2) \approx ?$.
